    "md5": "1ec0f61c65872e190f9b1a30cec3b315",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1733,
    "chunk_text": "\u201c \u2018Therefore, as public officers, the respondents were obliged, at all times, to act in a manner which honoured the trust reposed in them. We are of the view that by the actions, omissions, decisions and conduct hereinbefore identified to have demonstrably contributed to the economic crisis and we are of the view that the 2nd\u2026, 2A\u2026, 28th\u2026, 29th\u2026, 30th\u2026, 31st\u2026, 32nd\u2026, 32A\u2026 and 38th\u2026 respondents had violated the Public Trust reposed in them and we hold that they were in breach of the fundamental right to equal protection of the law ordained by Article 12(1) of the Constitution.\n\n\"It may be noted that a violation of a Fundamental Right is not tantamount to a conviction before a court of law. The Supreme Court has determined that the standard of proof in Fundamental Rights Applications \u2018is usually expressed as involving the preponderance of probability\u2019. The standard of proof for a conviction before a court of law is \u2018beyond reasonable doubt\u2019. The Supreme Court has also determined that the award of compensation in a Fundamental Rights Application amount to an acknowledgement of regret and a solatium for the hurt caused by the violation of a fundamental right and not as a punishment for duty, disregarded or authority abused. (Saman vs Leeladasa 1989 1 SLR 1).\n\nAccordingly, any steps that may be taken with regard to criminal or civil liability in pursuance to the aforesaid judgment should be in accordance with the applicable law and procedure established thereunder.\n\nFor ease of reference the judgment of the Supreme Court in SC/FR/195/2022 and SC/FR/212/2022 can be found on the Supreme Court website\u2026\u201d\n\n\u0db8\u0db8 \u0daf\u0dda \u0daf\u0dbd\u0dd3\u0db8\u0dca\u200d\u0db1\u0dba \u0dc3\u0db7\u0dcf\u0d9c\u0dad* \u0d9a\u0dcf\u0dbb\u0db1\u0dd9\u0dcf."
  },
