    "md5": "6837608ad74205092e9b207307ee6590",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1998,
    "chunk_text": "Sir, it is time to look at this issue I am going to raise next. There has been an attempt to import transmission pipes, distribution pipes for a length of almost 4,000 kilometres! All of that is now lying idle in the Colombo Port, strewn all over the place for the past several years. When this Government came to power, they went on a very ambitious programme to have almost a 78 per cent water supply coverage in the entire country. It is a very impossible thing to do. For the past 70 years, we have been able to barely cover about 50 per cent of the country's need. Within five years, they were attempting to cover almost 24 per cent more. For 70 years, we were able to cover only about 50 per cent of the pipe-borne water supply in the length and breadth of the country. So, there was this ambitious programme.\n\nNow, the banks have refused to pay the LCs. So, the demurrage is accumulating. If you take the demurrage, it will be several times more than the value of the pipes which are lying in the yards of the Sri Lanka Ports Authority. So, this is the serious problem in which the total inefficiency and that utterly unrealistic, ambitious programme have landed the country and the National Water Supply and Drainage Board. So, this has to be looked at. Of course, there are programmes possible and certain excess capacities in our treatment plants. May be so, but then, all of this could have been avoided, if there had been some thought about the impossibility of such targets they have set up.\n\nThen, it is also important for me to talk about some of the pending projects. To carry out the Pali-Aru Project, first, you have to put up a reservoir. You have only allocated a meagre sum of Rs. 250 million. That is hardly enough to do anything, leave alone a project. Even for a feasibility study, it may not suffice. We already have a problem with the Iranamadu Tank. In supplying water to the North, there had been problems with Kilinochchi farmers. We have had problems also in the past."
  },
