    "chunk_text": "to the climate changes. \uf0b7 Implementation of groundwater recharging projects.\n\n\uf0b7 Rainwater harvesting programme\n\n\uf0b7 Strengthening, monitoring and regulating of CBOs for their\n\nsustainability. \uf0b7 Capacity development of Departmental officials.\n\nPraja Jala Abhimani Programme\n\n- Implementation of new water supply schemes\n\n- Started 367\n\n- Completed 134 schemes\n\n- Provide safe drinking water to over 18,000 families all over the country.\n\n- Ongoing 233 projects Islandwide. In all these, the civil constructions have been completed. Due to economic crisis and the budgetary limitations, completion has gone beyond the plan but, some of the projects will be completed in year 2023.\n\nRehabilitation and improvement of existing water supply schemes\n\n- Will be completed 172.\n\nCKDu Prevention Programme\n\n- 205 of Nanofilters have been established by the Department so far in 13 districts which are affected by CKDu\n\n- Among this, 50 nano-treatment plants are to be established this year in northern area (Kilinochchi, Vavuniya and Mullaitivu) and 30 have been already completed. All the 50 nano-treatment plants will be handed over to the community this year.\n\nSanitation Programme\n\n- Individual sanitation and common sanitations will be provided to low-income holders especially living in catchment areas with the objective of maintaining the safety of water.\n\n- 1,193 Individual sanitation projects have been already handed over.\n\nCatchment Protection Programme\n\n- Tree planting programme in catchments\n\n- Over 200,000 plants have been planted in last few years\n\nImplementation of Water Safety Plans to mitigate or adaption to the climate changes and groundwater recharging projects.\n\n- 2,500 Water Safety Plans have been done in CB-based water supply schemes\n\nRainwater Harvesting Programme\n\n- Providing rainwater harvesting structures to households living in very remote areas for water.\n\n- So far 1,498 have been completed.\n\nYear 2024 Plan of Department of National Community Water Supply"
